/E ( Through sanitation, improved quality of life, reduced child mortality, stable economies, and modern healthcare, average life expectancies have increased dramatically in developing countries. In the EU, the average life expectancy in the 1960s was 69 years. In 2010, it increased to 80 years. Meanwhile, global life expectancy will increase to 78.1 in 2050, with drastic consequences for healthcare, world economies, population crises, resource depletion, and social systems \(Brown, 2015\).)
