91 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 54 0 R
/E (of life for the elderly. Ageing is a result of cellular decline, as telomere attrition and eventual cellular senescence lead to the loss of genome stability in the cell. Recent studies have shown that acute stress is a significant factor in ageing, as it causes dysregulation in the HPA axis, leads to increased cortisol levels, high oxidative stress, and suppressed telomerase activity. Once cellular ageing is accelerated, chronic diseases and dysregulated functionality pave the path for morbidity. This article reviews the relationship between psychological stress and telomere biology, highlighting stress management as a potential strategy for slowing cellular ageing. By emphasising healthspan over lifespan, it argues that interventions at the cellular level may provide a sustainable pathway to healthier ageing. Biotechnological interventions and epigenetic advancements may be our best hopes for the future.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
