/T ( This article aims to examine aging at the cellular level, focusing on telomere biology, the role of psychological stress, and the emerging evidence that stress management may slow telomere shortening, thus playing a pivotal role in promoting a longer and healthier lifespan. I will further emphasize the argument that slowing aging should not be seen as a privilege, but a biological necessity to achieve sustainability in our shared future.)
/E ( This article aims to examine aging at the cellular level, focusing on telomere biology, the role of psychological stress, and the emerging evidence that stress management may slow telomere shortening, thus playing a pivotal role in promoting a longer and healthier lifespan. I will further emphasize the argument that slowing aging should not be seen as a privilege, but a biological necessity to achieve sustainability in our shared future.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
99 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 56 0 R
/T (CELLULAR AGING AND TELOMERIC ATTRITION  Aging, a long-existing dilemma of all life around us, is the decline in physiological functionality associated with several hallmarks: greying hair, wrinkling skin, loss of muscle mass, weakening of bones, and thickening of blood vessels. Yet aging is a cellular process \(nowhere near merely physical manifestations\), occurring within cells that are just a few micrometres wide. Age is found to be accelerated by several cellular disabilities: exhaustion of ILS pathways, exhaustion of target of rapamycin, decline in sirtuin activity, mitochondrial dysfunction, excessive senescent cells, chronic inflammation, and collapse of the proteostasis mechanism \(Campisi et al., 2019\).)
/E (CELLULAR AGING AND TELOMERIC ATTRITION  Aging, a long-existing dilemma of all life around us, is the decline in physiological functionality associated with several hallmarks: greying hair, wrinkling skin, loss of muscle mass, weakening of bones, and thickening of blood vessels. Yet aging is a cellular process \(nowhere near merely physical manifestations\), occurring within cells that are just a few micrometres wide. Age is found to be accelerated by several cellular disabilities: exhaustion of ILS pathways, exhaustion of target of rapamycin, decline in sirtuin activity, mitochondrial dysfunction, excessive senescent cells, chronic inflammation, and collapse of the proteostasis mechanism \(Campisi et al., 2019\).)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0]
