/P 58 0 R
/E (The biotechnological interventions mentioned below are still in the development stage, requiring further refinement, but they are our best hope for the future. Bioengineered drugs such as mifepristone can block glucocorticoid receptors, therefore hindering cortisol attachment to the receptors. As a result, cortisol levels due to acute stress may increase, causing minimal damage to cells. As a consequence of dysfunctionality of the HPA axis, increased cortisol levels and high oxidative stress inhibit the enzymatic activity of telomerase, an enzyme responsible for the maintenance of telomere length.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 58 0 R
/E (Through gene therapy, the restoration of telomerase reverse transcriptase \(TERT\) has shown chromosomal longevity. Studies show reversal of tissue degeneration in mice, proving its importance in anti-aging. Additionally, the transfer of the TRF1 gene, essential for telomere replication, can be another method of achieving longevity through biotechnological methods. When TRF1 was integrated into the mice genome using AAV9, it was expressed in various organs: muscles, heart, brain, and liver. Another important discovery was that the rate of increase in cancer did not rise, indicating its further use via genetic engineering \(Hong and Yun, 2019\).)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
